Hours Worked: 10.5 hours 
Project: Section 26 — Lagrangian Particle Tracking (MW–M31 Merger Simulation)

Objective:
Implement and execute a full Lagrangian particle tracking pipeline on MW–M31 N-body simulation snapshots to study:
- radial migration (Δr)
- MW vs M31 mixing evolution
- particle trajectory morphology
- dynamical heating via path length and phase-space evolution

---------------------------------------------------------------------

WORK COMPLETED

1. Pipeline Integration
- Adapted a pre-built GenAI (Claude) template into a working analysis pipeline
- Integrated MW and M31 snapshot data across full time series (801 snapshots)
- Ensured compatibility with COM-centered coordinate system and simulation file structure
- Connected with earlier Eulerian diagnostics framework

---------------------------------------------------------------------

2. Particle Tracking System
Implemented dual-mode tracking:

A) ID-based tracking (if particle IDs exist in snapshots)
B) Phase-space KD-tree fallback tracking:
   - 6D space: (x, y, z, vx, vy, vz)
   - velocity scaling applied for distance metric consistency
   - nearest-neighbour matching between consecutive snapshots

Added robustness features:
- missing particle detection ("lost" particles via distance threshold)
- continuity via previous-state tracking buffer
- reduced dataset via sampling (~1200 tracked particles total)

---------------------------------------------------------------------

3. Sampling Strategy
Defined stratified initial-condition sampling at snapshot 0:

- Inner region: r₀ < 10 kpc (disk/bulge)
- Mid region: 10–50 kpc (inner halo)
- Outer region: r₀ > 50 kpc (outer halo)
- M31-origin tagged particles

Purpose: ensure representation across dynamical regimes.

---------------------------------------------------------------------

4. Trajectory Construction
Built full time-series arrays:

- r(t), x(t), y(t), z(t)
- v(t), radial velocity v_r(t)
Derived metrics:
- Δr = r_f − r₀
- path length L = ∫|dr|
- phase-space trajectories (r, v_r)

Optimized memory via incremental path length computation.

---------------------------------------------------------------------

